# ANTI-REGRESSION POSITIVE — C4, the intra-function SEVER.
#
# A `.get()`-chain view bound to a local, then the collection mutated, then the
# local read. The bind SEVERS the view from the collection — `s` holds the
# pre-mutation value and stays valid across the realloc:
#
#     String s = v.get(0).unwrap()
#     grow(&v)                       # reallocs the backing store
#     print(s)                       # must print the PRE-MUTATION value
#
# CORRECT TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, C backend, `--sanitize`): accepts,
# ASan-clean, prints "alpha-heap-forced-payload".
# ORACLE (ggdef, measured this session): identical — accepts, same output.
#
# This is the over-rejection guard for the whole `sound_excl_*` family. Those
# fixtures demand a REJECT when an element view and a writer of its collection
# are LIVE AT THE SAME CALL. Here they are in SEPARATE statements with an
# ownership transfer in between, which is legal and must stay legal — a
# chokepoint that rejects on "a view of `v` exists anywhere in scope" instead of
# "a view of `v` is live at this call" would break this program.
#
# The 4096-push loop FORCES a real reallocation, so a regression that turned the
# bind back into a borrow would surface as a sanitizer trip, not a silent pass.
void grow(Vector[String] &v):
    int i = 0
    while i < 4096:
        v.push("filler-padding-to-force-a-real-reallocation")
        i = i + 1

void main():
    Vector[String] v = Vector[String]()
    v.push("alpha-heap-forced-payload")
    String s = v.get(0).unwrap()
    grow(&v)
    print(s)
